      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.581,
            0.049,
            0.861,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Some Geometric Themes"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.089,
            0.745,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": "5. Find the number of cubes in this stack of identical cubes."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.12,
            0.612,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_27_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.32,
            0.802,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "6. What are the different shapes the projection of a cube can make under different orientations?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.812,
            0.316,
            0.905,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_27_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.374,
            0.416,
            0.395
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Isometric Projections"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.405,
            0.847,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "In general we lose information when projecting a solid to a plane. But depending on the orientation of the solid, we can sometimes recover much of the information we’ve lost."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.46,
            0.847,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider an orientation of a cube in which the lengths of the projections of all the edges of the cube are equal. Such a projection is called the **isometric** projection of the cube. ‘Isometric’ means ‘equal measure’ in Greek."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.533,
            0.846,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": "Imagine balancing a cube perfectly on one of its corner vertices, and then projecting it down to the floor plane. This projection is isometric and appears as shown."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.429,
            0.584,
            0.591,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_27_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.735,
            0.807,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "？ Construct a model of a cube and use your hands to keep it balanced on one corner vertex. Can you try to understand why all the projected edges have equal length?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.733,
            0.903,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_27_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.804,
            0.689,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "The isometric representation of a cube is a regular hexagon. If the cube is made of glass, then all the edges would be visible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.859,
            0.691,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": "This structure is the basis for **isometric** drawing! Tile the plane with hexagons and we get an **isometric grid**."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.698,
            0.782,
            0.853,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
